window.docContents[159].resolve({"/Normalizations/#Weil-and-Silverman-Height-Comparisons--Normalizations":{"contents":"Mathlib's logHeight₁ is the relative height: its local sum is naturally\nscaled by . Dividing by the degree produces the absolute height\nused in Silverman's comparison and makes the normalization insensitive to the\nfield over which an algebraic number is viewed. Nonnegativity descends from the\nnonnegative relative height.Status: Heights.normalizedLogHeight and\nHeights.normalizedLogHeight_nonneg are formalized unconditionally.\n\nFor a nonzero integral ideal ,  is defined using\nIdeal.absNorm. The accompanying lemmas prove strict positivity of the norm\nand nonnegativity of its logarithm from .Status: formalized unconditionally.\n\nMathlib normalizes the discriminant as ,\nwhereas the discriminant in the archimedean metric formula carries the factor\n. Retaining this factor gives the exact Proposition 1.1\nnormalization rather than merely a height differing by an additive constant;\nnonvanishing follows from nonvanishing of both factors.Status: Heights.silvermanModularDiscriminant and its nonvanishing theorem are\nformalized unconditionally.\n\nThe quotient  has leading term  and is therefore the\n modular invariant used in the height estimates, with no\nextra factor of . The  identity checks this convention,\nwhile modular invariance permits a preimage to be moved into the standard\nfundamental domain.Status: formalized unconditionally. Surjectivity is complemented by a proof\nthat equal values lie in one  orbit; the latter uses\nhomothety and rigidity of the associated period lattices. Consequently the\nPetersson-normalized absolute discriminant depends only on .\nprop:modular-estimates proves the required global bounds.\n\n","context":"Weil and Silverman Height Comparisons","header":"2. Normalizations","id":"/Normalizations/#Weil-and-Silverman-Height-Comparisons--Normalizations"}});